import{_ as a,o as t,c as n,a3 as i}from"./chunks/framework.DAK02U1-.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Compile Task Graph and Module Compilation","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Compile Task Graph and Module Compilation | clice.io"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://docs.clice.io/clice/design/task-graph"}],["meta",{"property":"og:locale","content":"en_US"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://docs.clice.io/clice/design/task-graph"}],["meta",{"name":"twitter:card","content":"summary"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://docs.clice.io/clice/design/task-graph"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://docs.clice.io/clice/design/task-graph"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://docs.clice.io/zh/clice/design/task-graph"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"clice/design/task-graph.md","filePath":"en/clice/design/task-graph.md","lastUpdated":1794738697000}'),s={name:"clice/design/task-graph.md"};function o(r,e,d,l,h,c){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="compile-task-graph-and-module-compilation" tabindex="-1">Compile Task Graph and Module Compilation <a class="header-anchor" href="#compile-task-graph-and-module-compilation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Compile Task Graph and Module Compilation&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="background" tabindex="-1">Background <a class="header-anchor" href="#background" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Background&quot;">​</a></h2><p>C++20 modules end independent compilation: before <code>main.cpp</code> can compile <code>import math;</code>, the interface unit of <code>math</code> must be compiled into a PCM, and a chain of imports must be compiled bottom up. Build systems handle this graph by scanning everything and compiling in order. A language server cannot:</p><ul><li><strong>It must answer in milliseconds</strong>: compile only the modules the open file needs, not the project&#39;s whole graph.</li><li><strong>Changes cascade</strong>: saving a module interface makes every PCM above it stale, mid-build or not.</li><li><strong>Requests overlap</strong>: several files need the same PCM, which must build once; a build nobody needs any more should stop.</li><li><strong>Code being typed can be wrong</strong>: a cycle of imports must be an error, not a deadlock.</li></ul><p>clangd&#39;s module support has lacked a design of its own for years — its own summary concluded that &quot;a design and new infrastructure is needed&quot; (<a href="https://github.com/clangd/clangd/issues/1293" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">clangd#1293</a>) — and sharing PCMs with the build system locks files against it (<a href="https://github.com/clangd/clangd/issues/2292" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">clangd#2292</a>). clice builds every expensive product on one <strong>compile task graph</strong>: lazily built, compiled on demand, cancelled cooperatively, invalidated along its dependencies. Modules are its richest client and the running example here.</p><h2 id="design" tabindex="-1">Design <a class="header-anchor" href="#design" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="nodes" tabindex="-1">Nodes <a class="header-anchor" href="#nodes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Nodes&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The graph knows nodes, edges, interest, attempts and invalidation, and nothing of what a node builds. A node&#39;s kind gives its meaning:</p><ul><li><strong>PCM</strong>: a module under a command it is built with; its edges are its imports, and the precompiled header its target reads.</li><li><strong>PCH</strong>: a preamble&#39;s content under its command — files whose preambles and commands agree share one node and one build.</li><li><strong>AST</strong>: an open document&#39;s compile, whose generation is the document&#39;s freshness.</li><li><strong>Unit</strong>: a translation unit compiled for its index or its lint.</li></ul><p><strong>Whoever waits on a node carries how to build it.</strong> A request, or a build waiting on a dependency, passes the node&#39;s work along; a waiter finding the node in need of a build with none running starts one with its work. Every waiter passes equivalent work, so it does not matter whose runs, and what a build needs lives exactly as long as someone waits for it — nothing is stashed for a build that may never start.</p><h3 id="lazy-construction" tabindex="-1">Lazy Construction <a class="header-anchor" href="#lazy-construction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lazy Construction&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A node is created when a request first reaches it, and each build resolves its dependencies anew, so opening a file scans and builds only the modules it reaches. An import no unit provides yet becomes a <strong>placeholder</strong> node, never built, only depended upon; when a provider appears, the placeholder is invalidated and its dependents rebuild against the real module.</p><h3 id="interest" tabindex="-1">Interest <a class="header-anchor" href="#interest" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Interest&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Interest counts how many active requests care about a node: a request acquires it while it waits and releases it when it finishes or is cancelled. A background build that a foreground request joins becomes foreground, and so do the builds it waits on. A build waiting on its dependencies holds interest in them too, so a module another module needs keeps building after the request that started it went away. At zero, the build is cancelled — after a short delay, since a request superseded by an edit releases its interest just before its successor takes it again.</p><h3 id="attempts" tabindex="-1">Attempts <a class="header-anchor" href="#attempts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Attempts&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each build is an attempt; concurrent requests join the running one. It ends <strong>succeeded</strong>, <strong>failed</strong> (an error, a cycle) or <strong>stale</strong> (overtaken by a change, or by nobody wanting it). Failure is not sticky: the node stays dirty, so the next request after the user fixes the code checks it again — while the failed build&#39;s inputs are unchanged it fails again without running, and once one changes or an importer is saved it builds again.</p><p>A clean node&#39;s product can still vanish or go stale behind the graph&#39;s back — an evicted PCH, an evicted AST, a file changed before any event says so — so the work also carries a <strong>check</strong>, asked when a waiter reaches a clean node: the product <strong>holds</strong>; the product is <strong>gone</strong> but what it was built from is not, and only this node builds again; or the <strong>inputs changed</strong>, and the node and its dependents are invalidated.</p><h3 id="cancellation-and-generations" tabindex="-1">Cancellation and Generations <a class="header-anchor" href="#cancellation-and-generations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cancellation and Generations&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Cancellation is advisory: a stop flag the build checks at declaration boundaries inside the compile, after which the attempt still reports a real result. A build is never torn down mid-flight, so the end of a build — write the artifact, then register it — can never be left half done.</p><p>Each node has a generation, raised by every invalidation that reaches it. A build records it when it starts and compares when it ends: a difference means something it read changed meanwhile, and its result is stale. The master is single-threaded, so this is all the race detection it needs.</p><h3 id="edges-and-cascades" tabindex="-1">Edges and Cascades <a class="header-anchor" href="#edges-and-cascades" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Edges and Cascades&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A node&#39;s edges are what its sources declare and what its last successful attempt waited on. A module&#39;s import scan declares its imports before it builds, and they stay its edges through a failed build, or fixing an import could never reach the module that failed on it. When a module file is saved, the graph walks reverse edges: every node above is marked dirty, and the running attempts that read what changed are overtaken. Before waiting on a dependency, the graph follows the chain of builds in progress and fails at once if it leads back to the waiter: a cycle is an error.</p><h3 id="resolving-imports" tabindex="-1">Resolving Imports <a class="header-anchor" href="#resolving-imports" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Resolving Imports&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The startup scan gives a registry of module names and the files declaring them (see <a href="./dependency-scanning">Dependency Scanning</a>); when several units declare one name, an import resolves to the nearest, and the importer is warned of the others. A build&#39;s own import scan runs the preprocessor on the unit as its command compiles it, skipping units that can import nothing and reusing a scan while the command and the files are unchanged — an open file&#39;s while its directive lines are, so typing elsewhere scans nothing. It runs on the buffer being edited, so an <code>import</code> typed but not saved gets its PCM built before the parse needs it.</p><p>A build system may compile one module several ways — once per set of importers&#39; flags, once per build directory — and Clang reads a PCM only into a compile with matching language options. An import therefore reads the build its importer&#39;s compile matches: one under an entry of the importer&#39;s own database first, as the real build would, and the module&#39;s own command among those.</p><h3 id="pcm-cache" tabindex="-1">PCM Cache <a class="header-anchor" href="#pcm-cache" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;PCM Cache&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each module keeps a record of its last build under each command: its key, what it read, and how it ended. The key names the build by configuration — the module, the compiler, the flags, the keys of the modules it imports — so a command change anywhere below a module changes its key; content changes are caught by checking what the build read with the shared file check (see <a href="./incremental-parse">Incremental Compilation</a>). Every build writes a new file in clice&#39;s own cache directory, so a rebuild never overwrites a PCM a compile is reading, and no file is shared with the build system. The records of successful builds are saved with the index, so a restart starts warm.</p><h2 id="faq" tabindex="-1">FAQ <a class="header-anchor" href="#faq" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FAQ&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Why interest instead of a task queue?</strong> A queue cannot say that nobody needs a build any more. Interest makes cancellation follow demand, not timeouts.</li><li><strong>Why cooperative cancellation?</strong> Destroying a build mid-flight makes every suspension point an exit, and the end of a build must never be split. A stop flag lets it leave at known points and always report a result.</li><li><strong>Why does the waiter carry the work?</strong> A build needs inputs only its requester has — a buffer&#39;s text, a lint run&#39;s arguments. Kept in the project for whichever build starts next, they would need rules for when to keep and drop them, and outlive their requests.</li><li><strong>Why a separate PCM directory?</strong> Sharing the build system&#39;s PCMs locks files against it. The cost is disk space and a first build of each module.</li></ul><h2 id="known-limitations" tabindex="-1">Known Limitations <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-limitations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known Limitations&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A module never saved to disk cannot be built</strong>: there is no file to compile. Once it is saved, its placeholder is invalidated and the importers rebuild against it.</li><li><strong>The graph lives in memory.</strong> A restart rebuilds it lazily from the persisted records and the module registry.</li></ul>',31)])])}const g=a(s,[["render",o]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
